the offer of judgment statute is 768.79

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.000
the offer of judgment statute is 768.79
and the procedural rule sets the timing and the form of the proposal

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:22.000
if the defendant makes a proposal and the plaintiff rejects it and the judgment is at least 25 percent less than the offer

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
the defendant gets attorney's fees and costs incurred from the date of the offer. the proposal must be in writing and state that it is made under the statute.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:42.000
the proposal must also be specific about who it is made to. joint proposals to multiple parties have to apportion the amount, or they fail. courts read the statute strictly because it is in derogation of the common law.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:55.000
separately there is the sanctions statute, 57.105, for frivolous claims. you must serve the motion and give the 21 days safe harbor before you file it.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:10.000
during those 21 days the other side can withdraw the frivolous claim or defense, and if they do, no sanctions. if they don't, the court can award reasonable attorney's fees against the party and the lawyer.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:30.000
now switching gears to discovery. a party may serve up to 30 interrogatories including subparts without leave of court, and requests for admission are deemed admitted if you do not respond in time, so calendar them.
